{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Six years you shall sow your field, etc.</b> We have already said that this is a promise that the land will not deny its strength even if one sows six consecutive years and does not leave the land fallow after two years of sowing. And by way of reward it says, ״כִּי תָבֹאוּ אֶל הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר אֲנִי נוֹתֵן לָכֶם״ — for in truth I am the One giving you the land, and not by your own strength did you inherit the land. And there is reason to fear lest you err and say, 'The land is yours'; therefore I command you, ״וְשָׁבְתָה הָאָרֶץ שַׁבָּת לַה׳״ — not for the sake of adding strength to the land, but for the sake of HaShem, to reveal that He, blessed be He, is the master of the field. And by the merit of your letting it rest for the sake of HaShem, you will merit what is said: ״שֵׁשׁ שָׁנִים תִּזְרַע שָׂדֶךָ וְגוֹ׳ וְאָסַפְתָּ אֶת תְּבוּאָתָהּ״ — to exclude: if you do not let it rest for the sake of HaShem, then exile will be incurred and you will not sow even in the six years. And if you say that you will sow in any case, strangers will plunder your toil and you will not gather its produce, as it is written (Judges 6:3): ״וְהָיָה אִם זָרַע יִשְׂרָאֵל וְעָלָה מִדְיָן וַעֲמָלֵק וַיַּשְׁחִיתוּ״, and it is written (Deuteronomy 28:38): ״זֶרַע רַב תּוֹצִיא הַשָּׂדֶה וּמְעַט תֶּאֱסֹף״. And it says ״שָׂדֶךָ״ to exclude the field of others — to say that if you do not let it rest, then you will go into exile and be a servant to the nations and sow their field and not your field; but by the merit of shemittah you will sow your field and your vineyard. And further, it mentions ״שָׂדֶךָ״ to exclude the year of shemittah, in which the field is not your field but ownerless to all — for since in this year you acknowledge that the land belongs to HaShem, if so it is fitting that the poor of His people be sustained from it in this year, and you should have no advantage over them. And it says that by the merit of ״וְשָׁבְתָה הָאָרֶץ שַׁבָּת לַה׳״ you will merit that six years you shall sow your field; but if you wish to hold onto the fruits of the seventh year as the owner, then you will come to poverty, so that you will sell the field and it will no longer be your field but someone else's — according to the view of the Midrash (Tanchuma, Behar 1) which expounds the juxtaposition of the parashiot: ״כִּי יָמוּךְ אָחִיךָ״ follows one who does business with the fruits of the seventh year, whose end is to come to poverty and to sell his house and his field. And this resembles what they said, their memory for blessing (Ta'anit 6a): if the wild beast is consumed from the field, your livestock will be consumed in the house — for as long as the wild beast eats, then by the merit of charity your livestock too will have; but if you hold onto it as the owner, refusing to give to the wild beast in the field, then the coin will be consumed from the purse, so that you too will have nothing." "And for this reason it is said in proximity: ״וּבַשָּׁנָה הַשְּׁבִיעִית וְגוֹ׳ שָׂדְךָ לֹא תִזְרָע״ — it placed the field before the sowing, to say: lest you err and say that the field is yours and not HaShem's; therefore I command you not to sow, for by this you will know that it belongs to HaShem and not to you. ״וְאֶת עִנְּבֵי נְזִירֶךָ״ — the grapes you set aside for yourself and did not wish to give of them to others, by this scheme that you devised, thinking the vineyard is your vineyard; therefore I command you ״לֹא תִבְצֹר״ — for by this you will know that it is not fitting for you to hoard and to separate other people from them, but the hand of all shall be equal in them, so that you may know that the land belongs to HaShem and it is in His hand to withhold or to give it to whomever He wishes; and the poor of your people shall eat from a high table. And this is the reason for the Jubilee, of which it is said (Leviticus 25:23): ״וְהָאָרֶץ לֹא תִמָּכֵר לִצְמִיתֻת״ — for a person cannot sell something that is not his, for the earth and its fullness belong to HaShem, and He gave it to a person for a fixed period of fifty years for an ordinary, average person, from the day he turns twenty and pursues the acquisitions of this world until he turns seventy; therefore he cannot sell an inheritance for more than fifty years."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The citation from Ta'anit 6a is a paraphrase of the Talmudic discussion of Leviticus 25:7 ('that your livestock may eat'); the Kli Yakar renders it freely in the idiom of wild beast vs. livestock to suit his homiletic point about shemittah.", 1 "The wordplay on נָזִיר / נָזוֹר / לֹא תִבְצֹר — grapes set aside (nezirekha), to withhold (linzor), and 'you shall not withhold' (lo tibtzor) — is central to the argument; the translation preserves the root connection throughout."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:38:39.414454894Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}